ID: Ch2-Ask-Kate
TEXT:

"Kate, help!" I shout over the ringing clash of steel.

Kate stays locked in combat, her ice shields batting away flying blades without a break in her rhythm.

"Kate!" I bellow again. "They're overwhelming Daren!"

Her gaze snaps toward us. Her right hand extends in a blur, palm facing the flanker. A dense spire of frost forms instantly from her open hand.

She unleashes it in a flash. The ice spire punches straight through the flanker's skull, dropping him dead before he can even blink.

The father roars in horror, freezing at the sight of his son's corpse. Daren wastes no time: stepping inside the axe's reach, he takes the bearded man's head clean off in a single sweeping arc.

Across the clearing, the sword-caster freezes in disbelief. Before he can react, Kate drives an ice lance across the distance, impaling him through the chest and pinning him dead against the earth.

The slaughter ends as swiftly as it began. My chest heaves, sweat stinging my eyes, relieved only that we survived the butcher's yard.

choice("Continue", Ch2-Battle-end, v_current_scene = Ch2-Battle-end)


ID: Ch2-Battle-end
TEXT:

#if(v_ch1_stillwater == 1) {
Daren weaves white light over his lacerated forearm, his jaw set in a hard, accusatory scowl.

"Barry, we need to talk. You lied to me, and we came within inches of dying because of it. You're no stillwater, are you?"

"No," I admit, looking him in the eye. "I'm not."

"You aren't a local either. Otherwise the tournament ward would have triggered the instant we crossed paths."

He refers to the monitoring enchantment woven into every participant before teleportation. Contestants are barred from harming the native population of Varathia. The ward warns of nearby locals, suppresses accidental blows, and permanently disqualifies and teleports away anyone who attempts intentional violence against them. It also functions as a receiver for trial objectives.

A marvel of magical engineering—yet the organizers never bothered verifying whether their entrants were actually mages. Or perhaps they simply did not care.
}
#if(v_ch1_stillwater == 2) {
White light bathes Daren's lacerations as the flesh knits beneath his palms. He looks up, wiping sweat and blood from his forehead.

"Damn it, Barry! That was too close. We need a coordinated strategy before the next fight, or we won't last until nightfall."

"I won't argue with that," I reply.

He finishes the weave and walks over to Kate, studying her with open bewilderment.

"Kate, help me understand. Fighting like that, why on earth would you ask for an escort?"

"Everyone has vulnerabilities, healer," she replies, shaking frost from her cuffs. "Watching you struggle, I'd say you are weakest against fellow weapon enchanters. Just because I dismantled three thugs does not mean I can survive every threat on this continent alone. No one wins this tournament by themselves."

"Yes... I suppose you have a point."
}
choice("Continue", Ch2-Confession, v_current_scene = Ch2-Confession) if (v_ch1_stillwater == 1)
choice("Continue", Ch2-Battle-end2, v_current_scene = Ch2-Battle-end2) if (v_ch1_stillwater == 2)


ID: Ch2-Confession
TEXT:

"Then you are a registered contestant," Daren continues, folding his arms. "What could possibly compel an ordinary human to enter a death match against the most powerful mages in the world?"

I am after the exact same prize as everyone else, Daren, but speaking it aloud is dangerous.

The Magium.

The mythical wellspring of all magic in existence, concealed somewhere on the continent of Varathia. Every caster alive dreams of reaching it, convinced that tapping its power will multiply their strength a hundredfold and elevate them to living gods. But while popular belief insists the Magium responds only to trained sorcerers, my research revealed a deeper truth: its power is boundless enough to grant magical ability to an ordinary human.

For years, reaching Varathia was impossible. The continent sat isolated behind impenetrable barriers, accessible only by rare, heavily monitored royal invitations whose recipients returned with their memories of the route scrubbed clean.

Then came the proclamation. A worldwide summons from the kings of Varathia, inviting every mage on earth to compete for the ultimate prize: access to the Magium itself.

It was the only opening I would ever get.

"Why did you join this tournament, Barry?" Daren presses.

choice("Tell him the truth", Ch2-Truth2, v_current_scene = Ch2-Truth2, v_ch2_human_reason = 1)
choice("Tell him I was forced into it", Ch2-Forced2, v_current_scene = Ch2-Forced2, v_ch2_human_reason = 2)
choice("Tell him I'm looking for a cure for my sick sister", Ch2-Sister2, v_current_scene = Ch2-Sister2, v_ch2_human_reason = 3)


ID: Ch2-Truth2
TEXT:

"I'm after the same thing as you, Daren," I tell him. "I'm after the Magium. It's been my dream ever since I was little to become a mage, and the Magium can grant me that wish."

"Come now, Barry. You'll need a better fabrication than that."

He trails off, catching the unblinking conviction in my eyes.

"By the gods... you're serious. Barry, I admire your determination, but you cannot bluff your way through a tournament of archmages."

"If I might interrupt," Kate cuts in, stepping between us. "We need to clear out before someone finds us among these corpses and assumes we slaughtered the entire clearing."

Daren looks over at her, his brow furrowing.

"I still don't understand you, woman. Fighting like that, why would you ever need an escort?"

"Everyone has vulnerabilities, healer," she replies, shaking frost from her cuffs. "Watching you struggle, I'd say you are weakest against fellow weapon enchanters. Just because I dismantled three thugs does not mean I can survive every threat on this continent alone. No one wins this tournament by themselves."

"Yes... I suppose you have a point."

choice("Continue", Ch2-Battle-end2, v_current_scene = Ch2-Battle-end2)


ID: Ch2-Forced2
TEXT:

"I didn't come here by choice," I tell him. "Believe me, I would love nothing more than to leave this place and never look back. But that's not really an option for me at this point. All I can do now is try to survive until the end of the tournament."

Daren strokes his chin, mulling over the problem.

"Wait, Barry... there is another way."

"There is?"

"The tournament ward! If you attempt to harm a local, the enchantment disqualifies you and teleports you straight back to the mainland. You only need to find a native Varathian, throw a punch, and you're free!"

"I... had not considered that."

"Don't worry," Daren vows, clapping a hand to his chest. "I'll see you safely to the nearest city!"

"If I might interrupt," Kate cuts in, stepping between us. "We need to clear out before someone finds us among these corpses and assumes we slaughtered the entire clearing."

Daren looks over at her, his brow furrowing.

"I still don't understand you, woman. Fighting like that, why would you ever need an escort?"

"Everyone has vulnerabilities, healer," she replies, shaking frost from her cuffs. "Watching you struggle, I'd say you are weakest against fellow weapon enchanters. Just because I dismantled three thugs does not mean I can survive every threat on this continent alone. No one wins this tournament by themselves."

"Yes... I suppose you have a point."

choice("Continue", Ch2-Battle-end2, v_current_scene = Ch2-Battle-end2)


ID: Ch2-Sister2
TEXT:

"It's... my sister," I say, dropping my gaze. "She is terminally ill, and the cure for her illness can only be found on this continent. I've tried for months to get an invitation to this place, but they would not hear of it. This was the only way. I can only hope that I'll somehow manage to survive long enough to find what I'm looking for."

Daren places a hand on my shoulder.

"We'll find it together. I give you my word: no harm will come to you while I draw breath."

"If I might interrupt," Kate cuts in, stepping between us. "We need to clear out before someone finds us among these corpses and assumes we slaughtered the entire clearing."

Daren looks over at her, his brow furrowing.

"I still don't understand you, woman. Fighting like that, why would you ever need an escort?"

"Everyone has vulnerabilities, healer," she replies, shaking frost from her cuffs. "Watching you struggle, I'd say you are weakest against fellow weapon enchanters. Just because I dismantled three thugs does not mean I can survive every threat on this continent alone. No one wins this tournament by themselves."

"Yes... I suppose you have a point."

choice("Continue", Ch2-Battle-end2, v_current_scene = Ch2-Battle-end2)


ID: Ch2-Battle-end2
TEXT:

Daren nods toward the treeline.

"Very well. Let's keep moving," he says, taking the lead and scanning the ridge above.

I turn to follow, but Kate steps into my path, catching the collar of my tunic in a tight, frozen fist. Her eyes narrow into dark slits, her knuckles cold enough to seep through the linen.

"Stillwater," she says in a low rasp, pitched well below Daren's hearing. "I saw you 'fighting' back there. You may have managed to convince Daren and that bearded fool that you are an ordinary human, but I am not so easily deceived. I don't know what your game is, but I am keeping my eye on you."

"I'm not—" I begin, but she twists the fabric, pulling me a fraction of an inch closer.

#if(v_ch2_kate_debt == 0) {
"Listen to me, stillwater. I'm not interested in your lies. I just wanted to make sure you were clear on what I think of your little charade. Now, are you clear?"
}
#if(v_ch2_kate_debt == 1) {
"You owe me, stillwater. I saved your life during that battle and you owe me. And I intend to collect that debt sooner rather than later. Are we clear?"
}
choice("Crystal.", Ch2-Crystal, v_current_scene = Ch2-Crystal) if (v_ch2_kate_debt == 0)
choice("Not really, could you elaborate some more?", Ch2-Elaborate, v_current_scene = Ch2-Elaborate) if (v_ch2_kate_debt == 0)
choice("Don't worry, I always pay my debts.", Ch2-Always, v_current_scene = Ch2-Always) if (v_ch2_kate_debt == 1)
choice("Yes, we're clear.", Ch2-Clear, v_current_scene = Ch2-Clear) if (v_ch2_kate_debt == 1)
choice("I don't owe you anything.", Ch2-No-debt, v_current_scene = Ch2-No-debt, v_ch2_not_owe_debt = 1) if (v_ch2_kate_debt == 1)


ID: Ch2-Crystal
TEXT:

"Crystal," I tell her.

"Good," she says, releasing my collar with a sharp push. "Now move. We need to catch up to Daren."

choice("Continue", Ch2-Deer, v_current_scene = Ch2-Deer)


ID: Ch2-Elaborate
TEXT:

"Not really, could you elaborate some more?" I ask her.

Her dark eyes flash with razor-sharp irritation, her fingers tightening on my collar for one warning heartbeat.

"Joke all you want, stillwater, but I've got my eye on you."

She shoves me back and strides ahead to catch up with Daren, leaving cold air in her wake.

choice("Continue", Ch2-Deer, v_current_scene = Ch2-Deer)


ID: Ch2-Always
TEXT:

"Don't worry," I tell her. "I always pay my debts."

The hostility in her posture eases by a bare fraction. She searches my face, weighing the promise, before her fingers unclamp from my linen.

"Very well, then," she says, stepping back onto the trail. "Let's be off. We need to catch up to Daren."

choice("Continue", Ch2-Deer, v_current_scene = Ch2-Deer)


ID: Ch2-Clear
TEXT:

"Yes, we're clear," I tell her.

She holds my gaze for a second longer, making sure the point landed, then releases my collar with a curt nod.

"Very well, then," she says. "Let's be off. We need to catch up to Daren."

choice("Continue", Ch2-Deer, v_current_scene = Ch2-Deer)


ID: Ch2-No-debt
TEXT:

"I don't owe you anything," I tell her. "You only helped me because you saw that Daren was getting killed."

Kate's jaw locks, stepping hard into my space, her breath cold against my chin.

"You were the one getting killed, stillwater. Not Daren. And you will pay your debt."

She shoves me violently back into the brush, releasing my collar, and marches after Daren without another word.

choice("Continue", Ch2-Deer, v_current_scene = Ch2-Deer)


ID: Ch2-Deer
TEXT:

set(v_ch2_deer_interaction,0)
set(v_ch2_understood_deer,0) if (v_ancient_languages < 2)
set(v_ch2_understood_deer,1) if (v_ancient_languages >= 2)
#if(v_ancient_languages < 2) {
Near the dense tree line, brush rustles as a doe and her fawn emerge from the foliage, stepping cautiously across the moss-covered shale. Catching sight of us, they startle, let out soft grunts of alarm, and bolt back into the thick undergrowth.

We turn toward the mountain trail, searching the rocky slopes for a cave to shelter us for the night.
}
#if(v_ancient_languages >= 2) {
Near the dense tree line, brush rustles as a doe and her fawn emerge from the foliage, stepping cautiously across the moss-covered shale. As the young fawn bleats, the sounds resolve into fluent syllables of an ancient dialect, instantly translated by my stat booster.

"Mommy, there are more outlanders this way!"

"Don't worry, child," the fawn's mother tells him. "We'll find a safe way back to our cave. I promise."

They head back towards the woods.

"Wait," I call after them in their own language.

The two deer stop and look towards me.

"You can... understand us?" asks the doe.
}
